Day 244/250: Minimize Maximum Pair Sum in Array

Problem Statement #1877

Problem Statement Explanation:

The goal is to take an array of even length n and pair up all elements into n/2 pairs. 
We want to do this in a way that the maximum sum among all pairs is as small as possible.

Example:
Input: nums = [3, 5, 2, 3]
Output: 7

Explanation: The pairs are (2, 5) and (3, 3). The maximum sum is max(7, 6) = 7.

Solution Approach:

To minimize the maximum sum, we need to balance the values. 
If we pair the largest number with the second-largest number, the sum will be unnecessarily high. 
Instead, we should pair the smallest number with the largest number.

Step-by-Step Breakdown:
Sort the Array: By sorting the array in ascending order, we can easily identify the smallest and largest available elements.
Two-Pointer Pairing: Use two pointers (or a single loop with index math) to pair the element at index i with the element at index n−1−i.
Track the Maximum: Iterate through the first half of the array, calculate the sum for each pair, and keep track of the highest sum found.

Here's the code:

import java.util.Arrays;

class Solution {
    public int minPairSum(int[] nums)
        Arrays.sort(nums);
        
        int maxPairSum = 0;
        int n = nums.length;
        for (int i = 0; i < n / 2; i++) {
            int currentPairSum = nums[i] + nums[n - 1 - i];
            maxPairSum = Math.max(maxPairSum, currentPairSum);
        }
        
        return maxPairSum;
    }
}

Complexity:

Time Complexity:	O(nlogn)
Space Complexity:	O(1)

